              {
                "title": "Radical equation",
                "instruction": "An equation with a variable in a radicand or with a variable raised to a rational exponent."
              },
              {
                "title": "Steps",
                "instruction": "1. Isolate the radical (or the power) on one side. 2. Raise both sides to the index of the radical (the denominator of the exponent). 3. Solve the new equation. 4. CHECK every candidate in the ORIGINAL equation."
              },
              {
                "title": "Extraneous solution",
                "instruction": "Raising both sides to an even power can create a candidate that does not satisfy the original equation. It is an extraneous solution and is rejected."
              },
              {
                "title": "An even root is never negative",
                "instruction": "If an isolated even root equals a negative number, the equation has no real solution — a check will show it."
              }
            ]
          },
          "hints": [
            "Add 1 to both sides first.",
            "Squaring √(y − 2) leaves y − 2.",
            "Check your candidate in the ORIGINAL equation: the left side should come out to 5."
          ]
